const body = `<h1>9.5c &#8212; ShapeCollection: Polymorphic Operations</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Expand Application. The array worked for 3 shapes.
    Now build a collection that can hold any number of shapes, compute totals,
    and filter by type &#8212; all using polymorphism.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>Build a <code>ShapeCollection</code> that uses an ArrayList to store
    any mix of shapes. Add methods to compute total area, find the shape
    with the largest perimeter, and count how many of each type exist.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">=== SHAPE COLLECTION ===
Total shapes: 6
Total area: 283.64
Largest perimeter: Circle (radius 8.0) = 50.27
Circles: 2, Rectangles: 3, Triangles: 1</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Build It: ShapeCollection</h3>

    <p>Create <code>ShapeCollection.java</code>:</p>

    <pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ShapeCollection {
    private ArrayList&lt;Shape&gt; shapes;

    public ShapeCollection() {
        shapes = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    }

    public void add(Shape s) {
        shapes.add(s);
    }

    public double getTotalArea() {
        double total = 0;
        for (Shape s : shapes) {
            total += s.getArea();  // WHICH getArea()? Depends on the object
        }
        return total;
    }

    // YOUR CODE:
    // getLargestPerimeter() - returns the Shape with max perimeter
    // countByType() - returns how many Circles, Rectangles, Triangles
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The <code>getTotalArea()</code> loop calls
                <code>s.getArea()</code> on every shape. How does Java
                know which formula to use without any if-statements?</li>
            <li>What does the <code>instanceof</code> keyword do? Why
                might you need it for <code>countByType()</code>?</li>
            <li>Is <code>instanceof</code> a sign of good design or
                a code smell? When is it acceptable vs. when does it
                mean your parent class is missing methods?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker Challenge</h3>
        <p>Add a <code>printReport()</code> method that prints the
        full collection sorted by area (largest first). You'll need
        to adapt the selection sort from 7.3c to work with Shapes
        instead of Players. What changes?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <p>If you add a new shape type (like <code>Square</code> that
        extends <code>Rectangle</code>), does <code>countByType()</code>
        still work? A Square IS-A Rectangle, so <code>instanceof Rectangle</code>
        returns true for Squares too. Is that what you want? How would
        you distinguish between a Rectangle and a Square?</p>
    </div>
</div>

